<p class="tt-kicker"><span>Process</span> <span>Communication Model</span></p>
<h1 class="tt-title">A practical model<br class="tt-desktop-break"> for understanding<br class="tt-desktop-break"> how people communicate.</h1>
<p class="tt-hero-lead">Process Communication Model® (PCM) explains how people perceive the world, communicate, stay motivated and respond under stress. It helps us recognise individual differences and adapt the way we connect — without putting people into boxes.</p>
</div>
<figure class="tt-people">
<a class="tt-people-link" href="/six-personality-types/" aria-label="Explore the six PCM Personality Types">
<img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-six-ptypes-together-505x1024.webp" srcset="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-six-ptypes-together-505x1024.webp 505w, https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-six-ptypes-together-768x1556.webp 768w, https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-six-ptypes-together.webp 990w" sizes="(max-width: 850px) 82vw, 505px" width="505" height="1024" alt="Six illustrated people representing the six PCM Personality Types">

</a>
</figure>
<div class="tt-types-copy"><p class="tt-kicker">Personality structure</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">One personality.<br>Six Personality Types.</h2><div class="tt-body"><p>PCM does not place people into six separate boxes. It starts from the understanding that all six Personality Types are present in every one of us: <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-thinker" href="/six-personality-types/">Thinker</a>, <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-persister" href="/six-personality-types/">Persister</a>, <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-harmonizer" href="/six-personality-types/">Harmonizer</a>, <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-rebel" href="/six-personality-types/">Rebel</a>, <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-promoter" href="/six-personality-types/">Promoter</a> and <a class="tt-pcm-type tt-imaginer" href="/six-personality-types/">Imaginer</a>. This means we can find a point of connection with each of them in other people.</p><p>Each type brings its own strengths, way of perceiving the world, communication preferences and psychological needs. Their unique order and combination form our individual Personality Structure — which is why the same words, situations and relationships can feel so different to different people.</p><p>No type is better than another. PCM helps us recognise these differences and communicate in ways that are easier for others to understand.</p></div><a class="tt-button tt-types-link" href="/six-personality-types/" aria-label="Explore the six Personality Types">Explore the six Personality Types</a></div>
</div></div>
</section>

<section class="tt-origin">
<div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-origin-panel">
<div><p class="tt-kicker">The story behind the model</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">Where PCM came from</h2><div class="tt-history"><div class="tt-history-item"><strong>1970s</strong><span>Dr Kahler identifies observable patterns.</span></div><div class="tt-history-item"><strong>Late 1970s</strong><span><b>NASA</b> applies Kahler’s insights to astronaut selection.</span></div><div class="tt-history-item"><strong>1982</strong><span>Process Communication Model® is formally created.</span></div></div></div>
<div class="tt-body"><p>Process Communication Model® was developed by American clinical psychologist Dr Taibi Kahler in the 1970s. Through his research, he identified recurring and observable patterns in the way people communicate — and in how their behaviour changes under stress.</p><p>In the late 1970s, Dr Kahler applied these insights to help NASA select astronauts for missions. In 1982, he validated the Personality Pattern Inventory and formally created PCM: a practical framework for understanding personality differences and making communication more effective.</p></div>
<p class="tt-origin-clinton">In 1984, Hillary Clinton invited Dr Kahler to give a private three-day PCM seminar to Bill Clinton, then Governor of Arkansas, and a small group close to them. Dr Kahler later advised Clinton on connecting with different audiences and reviewed speeches during his presidential campaign.</p>
</div></div>
</section>

<section class="tt-works">
<div class="tt-container">
<div class="tt-works-head"><p class="tt-kicker">A practical framework</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">How PCM works</h2></div>
<div class="tt-step-grid">
<article class="tt-step"><div class="tt-step-visual"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-how-it-works-02.webp" width="1239" height="2048" loading="lazy" alt=""></div><h3>Be aware of your personality</h3><p>Understanding your preferences means knowing what energizes you and what ticks you off.</p></article>
<article class="tt-step"><div class="tt-step-visual"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-how-it-works-04.webp" width="1443" height="2048" loading="lazy" alt=""></div><h3>Recognize what other people do, say, and show</h3><p>These observations inform you about people’s preferences and the best way to connect.</p></article>
<article class="tt-step"><div class="tt-step-visual"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-how-it-works-01-1.webp" width="1254" height="2048" loading="lazy" alt=""></div><h3>Adapt your communication style</h3><p>In order to make a genuine connection that feels good for both of you.</p></article>
<article class="tt-step"><div class="tt-step-visual"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/pcm-how-it-works-03.webp" width="1424" height="2048" loading="lazy" alt=""></div><h3>Build stronger relationships</h3><p>With everyday interactions, at work and at home, every day.</p></article>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="tt-research">
<div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-research-panel">
<div class="tt-research-top"><div class="tt-research-heading"><p class="tt-kicker">Established and evolving</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">Grounded in research.<br>Used around the world.</h2></div><div class="tt-research-stats-area"><div class="tt-stats" aria-label="PCM worldwide"><div class="tt-stat"><strong>58</strong><span>countries</span></div><div class="tt-stat"><strong>1.8m+</strong><span>people</span></div></div></div></div>
<div class="tt-body"><p>Since its creation in 1982, PCM has continued to be researched and applied in a wide range of personal and professional contexts. Today, the model is practised in 58 countries, and more than 1.8 million people have experienced it.</p><p>PCM has been used by organisations including NASA, Pixar, L’Oréal, IKEA, BMW and Coca-Cola — as well as by people who want to understand themselves and communicate more effectively in everyday life.</p></div>
</div></div>
</section>

