</style><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"AboutPage","@id":"https://tujutaju.ee/about/#webpage","url":"https://tujutaju.ee/about/","name":"About Maia Mitšulis | Certified PCM Trainer in Estonia","inLanguage":"en","mainEntity":{"@id":"https://tujutaju.ee/#maia-mitsulis"},"isPartOf":{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://tujutaju.ee/#website","name":"Tujutaju","url":"https://tujutaju.ee/"}},{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://tujutaju.ee/#maia-mitsulis","name":"Maia Mitšulis","jobTitle":"Certified Process Communication Model trainer","description":"The first certified Process Communication Model trainer in Estonia, offering practical communication training and consultations.","url":"https://tujutaju.ee/about/","image":"https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TrainerMaiaMitsulis-1.webp","worksFor":{"@type":"Organization","@id":"https://tujutaju.ee/#organization","name":"Tujutaju","url":"https://tujutaju.ee/"},"homeLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Tallinn, Estonia"},"knowsLanguage":["English","Estonian","Russian"]}]}</script><div class="tt-page" id="tt-about-page"><main>
<section class="tt-hero"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-hero-grid"><div class="tt-hero-copy"><p class="tt-kicker">About Maia</p><h1 class="tt-title">Hello, I’m Maia Mitšulis.</h1><p class="tt-hero-lead">I’m Estonia’s first certified <a href="https://tujutaju.ee/pcm/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px">Process Communication Model® (PCM)</a> trainer.</p></div><figure class="tt-hero-art"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TrainerMaiaMitsulis-1.webp" alt="Maia Mitšulis holding a tablet during a Process Communication Model training session" width="800" height="987" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-origin"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-origin-panel"><figure class="tt-origin-photo-flow"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/TrainerMaiaMitsulis-1.webp" alt="Maia Mitšulis holding a tablet during a Process Communication Model training session" width="800" height="987" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><div><p class="tt-kicker">My path</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">From spaces to the space between people</h2></div><div class="tt-body"><p>I began in graphic design and later worked in interior design. I cared about making spaces beautiful, functional and comfortable for the people in them.</p><p>Eventually, I became more curious about the space between people: why one person hears support where another hears criticism, and why even a well-meant message can be understood differently from how it was intended. PCM brought structure to that curiosity. It helped me see how differences in perception, motivation and stress play out in conversation, and how our ways of communicating shape the relationships we build.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-belief" aria-label="On communication and relationships"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-belief-panel"><p class="tt-belief-label">My insight</p><p class="tt-belief-copy"><span>Not every conversation<span class="tt-insight-landscape-dash"></span><br class="tt-insight-landscape-break"> leads to a relationship <span class="tt-insight-original-dash">—</span></span><span>but every relationship is shaped by conversations.</span><span>More conscious communication can improve our relationships.</span></p></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-practice"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-practice-grid"><div class="tt-practice-head"><div><p class="tt-kicker">How I work</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">Understanding grows through experience.</h2><div class="tt-body"><p>PCM gives us precise language for personality, motivation, communication and stress. In my work, that language becomes most useful when people connect it to situations they recognise and have a chance to practise.</p><p>I make room for questions, experiment and humour. The aim is to leave with something you can notice and use in your next conversation, not simply a set of terms to remember.</p></div></div><figure class="tt-practice-photo"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/about-pcm-workshop-20261005.webp" alt="Participants taking part in a communication workshop" width="1206" height="795" loading="lazy"></figure></div><div class="tt-practice-side"><div class="tt-scene"><p class="tt-scene-label">A moment from a workshop</p><p>In <a href="https://tujutaju.ee/workshop/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:3px">a practical session on six ways of communicating</a>, participants tried each one for themselves. Afterwards, they described a shift: the six ways had become something they could <strong>experience</strong>, not just names on a slide.</p><p>They noticed that some ways of communicating came more easily than others. Some were surprised to find that the easiest way was different from the one they had expected. The exercise gave them a new perspective on their own preferences.</p></div><blockquote class="tt-voice"><p><span class="tt-voice-text-quote" aria-hidden="true">“</span>Special thanks for your energy and ability to explain complex things in simple language. It was not only informative, but also really interesting!”</p><footer>PCM training participant</footer></blockquote></div></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-principles"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-principles-head"><p class="tt-kicker">What matters to me</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">A precise model, made human</h2></div><div class="tt-principles-grid"><article class="tt-principle"><h3>Clarity</h3><p>To me, PCM is like a periodic table of human relationships. What first looks like endless variety begins to reveal a pattern, and that pattern helps us understand what happens between people.</p></article><article class="tt-principle"><h3>Practice</h3><p>PCM is a tool we can use in everyday conversations. That is what “a tool to make a daily difference” means to me: it helps us notice what is happening and gives us the option to choose a way of communicating that fits the person and the moment.</p></article><article class="tt-principle"><h3>Patterns of our own</h3><p>We all have the same six Personality Types, yet their different arrangements create a remarkable variety of personality structures. I find that shared foundation and the beauty of each individual pattern equally fascinating.</p></article></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-personal"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-personal-panel"><figure class="tt-personal-art"><img src="https://tujutaju.ee/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Tujutaju-about-Maia-Mitsulis-artwork.webp" alt="Collage of Maia Mitšulis’s creative work, including ceramics, photography and painting" width="1600" height="1300" loading="lazy"></figure><div class="tt-personal-copy"><p class="tt-kicker">Beyond training</p><h2 class="tt-section-title">Still making things</h2><div class="tt-body"><p>I love photography and the small details it invites me to notice. I pay attention to nuances in conversations, too. I also enjoy making things by hand, and I love to draw. I usually draw on the small pages of a sketchbook, but recently I surprised myself by painting an acrylic canvas about 1.1 by 1.5 metres.</p><p>I also play tennis, where I am on the other side of training. My coach notices what I need to develop and finds ways for me to practise through play. It reminds me how much attention and enjoyment matter when we learn.</p></div></div></div></div></section>
<section class="tt-next"><div class="tt-container"><div class="tt-next-panel"><div><h2 class="tt-section-title">Let’s get acquainted</h2></div><div class="tt-next-content"><p class="tt-next-intro">If something here resonates with you, I’d be glad to meet. In a free 20-minute introduction call, you can tell me what brought you here, ask me questions and get a sense of how I work. We can see together whether there is a useful next step.</p><div class="tt-next-links"><a class="tt-button" href="https://calendly.com/tujutaju/free-pcm-intro-call">Book a free 20-minute call</a></div></div></div></div></section>
</main></div><style id="tt-normalize-20261006-306">#tt-about-page .tt-hero-copy>.tt-kicker{font-family:Poppins,sans-serif!important;font-size:18px!important;font-weight:700!important;line-height:1.3!important;letter-spacing:.12em!important;text-transform:uppercase!important;margin:0!important}#tt-about-page .tt-hero-copy>.tt-title{margin:19px 0 0!important}#tt-about-page .tt-hero-lead{margin-top:12px!important}#tt-about-page .tt-next .tt-section-title{color:var(--blue,#626E98)!important}#tt-about-page .tt-button{border-radius:16px 0 16px 16px!important}@media(max-width:600px){#tt-about-page .tt-hero-copy{text-align:right!important}}</style>
